<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/foundational/epistemology">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Epistemology asks <strong>what knowledge is and where it comes from</strong> — how we know
    anything, from the existence of the external world to the accuracy of a news report. Its
    central analysis (knowledge = justified true belief) stood for 2,000 years until a three-page
    1963 paper broke it, and the repairs are still under construction.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the analysis, the Gettier explosion, scepticism, and the modern repair" widget="equationStep" config={epiCfg} />

  <h2>Rationalism vs empiricism — the classic divide</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Empiricists (Locke, Hume):</strong> knowledge comes from experience; the mind starts as a blank-ish page. Hume pushed to the edge: even causation is habit, not observation.</li>
    <li><strong>Rationalists (Descartes, Leibniz, Kant):</strong> some knowledge is built into the mind's structure. Kant's synthesis (1781): experience requires the mind's categories — space, time, causality — built in.</li>
    <li><strong>Modern cognitive science ruled on parts of it:</strong> infants arrive with expectations (the development lesson) — core knowledge exists, and Kant looks better than blank-slate empiricism did.</li>
    <li><strong>Why this matters practically:</strong> every field's evidential standards (statistics, law, journalism) are applied epistemology — the logic lesson and the statistics ladder are this subject's working children.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Gettier, Analysis (1963); Descartes, Meditations (1641); Kant, Critique of Pure Reason (1781); Goldman, "What Is Justified Belief?" (1979).</p>
</TopicPage>
